 we have a large  largely internationally based team you  know half of our team is based in  singapore but half our team is you know  just all around the world from a diverse  you know array of countries like jordan  india  um yeah like many many many countries  basically so  yeah so um how our model kind of works  is that um  we we actually run on a task-based um  system so um in in traditional you know  terminology is called the agile system  um for for programming so what that does  is that we have sprints right we have  periods of like intense work and we we  actually look at the results of of the  of the sprint itself so we don't assign  um into like tasks for people to to  complete we don't dictate you know when  do you start work when you end work i  think everything is task based and task  oriented and we are result oriented as  well so when we do it that way um i  think you know each each person in the  team has kind of an individual  responsibility but at the same time they  kind of get the freedom to work wherever  they want whenever they want as long as  the task is accomplished and as long as  they can communicate with the teammates  to you know kind of set a certain  working environment for each other  do you have a sense of in terms of hours  what the overall impact is accounting  for  task based rather than time based seven  days a week in principle but with  unlimited paid leave  does it sort of work out similarly to  normal jobs i mean what's the actual  impact  yeah so i think the flaw of the like the  five day or four day work week right is  the idea of quantifying work in hours or  in like time yeah um but most of the  time at the workplace you're not  spending all your eight hours you know  at work you're probably spending like  four hours in the toilet or you know  playing playing games on your phone and  stuff like that yeah so um i would say  you know the total amount of work being  done actually increases but the total  amount of productivity also increases  but the time maybe is what goes down  yes the the total time goes down that's  very interesting  last thing on this how long have you  been working in this paradigm  right so um in the  uh you know the defeat of influencer  marketing we started the sponsy project  last year in in 2020  but it was always an idea that um you  know  my fellow co-founders ruben and i had uh  we shared this vision you know since  2016 because we were utilizing  influencer marketing back then already  for a few of our other startups so i  think this is our largest project to  date um and you know it is also our most  promising and you know there's a there's  a long way to go and there's a lot of  opportunities available for us um so  yeah um this this is kind of the the  background of of everything that has has  been going on i guess  where is sponsy headed in terms of the  rest of this year what's what's the end  game what's the sort of big picture of  the road map  yeah so um if you you don't already know  um you can actually download the sponsor  app right now so um yeah it's actually  available on both app store and um  google play  we kind of did a a release of the um  last week just couple of days ago and  we're running a marketing campaign in  singapore right now and the theme of our  marketing campaign centers around  supporting local businesses because you  know there is like a pandemic situation  going on so a lot of small and  medium-sized businesses kind of lose out  to the larger tech corporations so what  we're trying to do is to kind of use  influencer marketing to give these other  businesses a boost um so yeah and in the  long run um you know this the spawn  project is not just about spawn c uh we  actually are planning and developing a  whole ecosystem of projects and  platforms so um we do have another you  know influencer platform for crypto  based influencers coming up it's called  chile  and we also have an nft marketplace that  is also in development right now so i  would say that the whole spawn project  itself um you know it's it's um it it  kind of encompasses a large array of  ecosystem projects  other than specifically  the influencer monetization and dispute  resolution i know you say there's a lot  of different projects can you talk about  some of the specific  uh  services or technologies that the  platform will make available for  influencers  yeah so this is about the sponsy  platform itself right yeah  yeah so um yeah so a couple of things we  are trying to do so i think the first  thing we want to say is that you know we  are not against um talent management  agencies or influencer agencies i think  they do play a huge role  and they have played a huge role in  bringing influencer marketing to the  masses so yeah they're not competitors  and we're actually trying to you know  get them to be our customers as well um  so we're developing our own backend  system that will allow like talent  agencies to manage all of their talents  and models or influences at once via a  single interface so they can chat with  you know all sorts of sponsors from a  single user interface itself um  we're also seeking to do an  international launch very very soon so  the purpose of the singapore launch is  basically you know firstly this is  tribute to our home country because both  rupert and i were from singapore and are  from singapore um yeah and um you know  the second thing is you know it kind of  gives us um  some some market um  market data to to improve our app before  we roll our international launch so i  think um in the in the long run what  features of this ponzi project uh will  you know shine through internationally  is of course um the the idea of the  contract between the influencer or what  we call the spawn c and the business  which is what we call the sponsor and  ultimately uh where we're going is that  we are not trying to label the users of  our apps as influencers we are trying to  create a new 